      "caseNotes": "\nPatient:\nMr Gordon Halliwell, 61, self-employed plumber.\nLives in a terraced house with his partner; no children.\nSeen 31 August 2026.\nSeen in the head and neck outpatient clinic, with his partner, for about an hour.\nReferred by the head and neck clinical nurse specialist following the multidisciplinary meeting.\n\nDiagnosis:\nT3 squamous cell carcinoma of the larynx.\nDiagnosed on biopsy in July 2026 after three months of hoarseness.\nThe multidisciplinary team on 26 August recommended total laryngectomy, planned in three weeks on 21 September 2026.\nNeck dissection also planned.\n\nHistory:\nForty pack-year smoking history; stopped at diagnosis six weeks ago.\nHe stopped without support and has not smoked since.\nAlcohol intake approximately eighteen units weekly, above the recommended low-risk limit of 14 units a week.\nAUDIT-C screening requested; the surgical and anaesthetic teams have been alerted to possible alcohol withdrawal after surgery.\n\nPast medical history:\nCOPD, moderate, FEV1 62 per cent predicted.\nUses his inhalers correctly; technique checked in clinic.\nHypertension.\nLast COPD review with the practice nurse in June 2026; no exacerbations in the past year.\nInguinal hernia repair in 2018.\nFractured left wrist after a fall from a ladder in 2012, fully healed.\nBlood pressure 138/84 in clinic.\nCholecystectomy in 2015, uncomplicated.\n\nMedication:\nTiotropium once daily.\nSalbutamol as required.\nAmlodipine 5 mg daily.\n\nAllergies:\nSulfa drugs — rash.\n\nCurrent status:\nHoarse but intelligible voice; he can still work by telephone.\nHis voice is weaker by the end of the day and on long calls.\nMild stridor on exertion, present for three weeks and worsening.\nSwallowing functional with mild sticking of solid food; no weight loss.\nHe has been choosing softer foods and cutting meat into small pieces.\nNo aspiration.\nWeight 82 kg, stable over the past three months.\nHearing and eyesight adequate; reads and writes English fluently, which will help with a writing board.\n\nPsychosocial:\nLives with his partner of twenty years.\nHis partner, Ms Janet Cole, works part-time as a school administrator and keeps the business accounts.\nShe is willing to attend all appointments with him.\nThey have two dogs and a caravan on the coast.\nAnxious about permanent voice loss and about the impact on his work and social life; he runs a two-man business and takes all the calls himself.\nRated his worry at eight out of ten when asked during the session.\nLimited understanding of post-surgery communication options — he believes he will be permanently mute, which is the main misconception to address.\nHe had not heard of a voice prosthesis before today.\nNo prior speech pathology contact.\nUses a smartphone confidently and sends text messages to customers.\nHe has not told his customers and is worried about losing the business.\nHe has read about laryngectomy online and found the material frightening.\nHolds a season ticket for the local football club.\nEnjoys sea fishing with friends most weekends.\nHe asked where to park for the pre-assessment clinic.\nHis apprentice, aged nineteen, could cover routine jobs for a few weeks.\n\nPlan:\nWorsening stridor reported to the head and neck surgical team on the same day, 31 August 2026, for airway review.\nPatient and partner advised to call 999 or attend the emergency department at once for stridor at rest, breathlessness or difficulty breathing.\nRequesting a pre-operative session, ideally within the next week so there is time for a second appointment.\nHis partner would like to attend, and both prefer a morning appointment.\nContent to cover: the anatomical change and its effect on voice, breathing and swallowing, including that he will breathe through a neck stoma permanently and will not be able to smell or blow his nose in the usual way.\nIntroduction of alaryngeal communication options — surgical voice prosthesis, electrolarynx and oesophageal speech — with recorded samples of each.\nEstablishment of a post-operative communication plan, including a writing board and an agreed signal system for the immediate post-operative period.\nBaseline voice and swallow measures to be recorded before surgery.\nHe agreed to a short voice recording for his own records before the operation.\nEarly post-operative review for stoma care and rehabilitation.\nHe was encouraged to hear that many people return to work after laryngectomy.\nHis COPD is noted, as it affects both the anaesthetic and later pulmonary rehabilitation through a stoma.\nHe is keen to maintain work where possible and this should shape the goals.\nHe would like to meet someone who has already had a laryngectomy, if this can be arranged.\nNo interpreter needed.\nPre-operative assessment clinic booked for 14 September 2026.\nHe asked whether his GP will be told about the plan; a copy of this letter is being sent to the practice.\n",
      "recipient": "The Head and Neck Speech Pathology Team, Metropolitan Cancer Centre",
