      "caseNotes": "\nPatient:\nMaster Kwame Mensah, 5 years 4 months.\nLives with his mother, father and older brother, aged eight.\nThe family spent three weeks in Ghana in July visiting relatives.\nStarting Year 1 in September 2026.\nTransfer of care from the community service, written 27 August 2026.\nGreenfield Primary School is the school he attended for Reception.\n\nDiagnosis:\nDevelopmental language disorder, receptive and expressive.\nDiagnosis explained to the family in April 2026; they understand it as a long-term difficulty with learning language.\n\nHistory:\nSeen by this community service for fourteen months, from June 2025.\nTherapy focused on following instructions, vocabulary and sentence building.\nReferred by the health visitor at the two-and-a-half-year check and re-referred at four.\nAt the first visit he used single words and a few two-word phrases.\nSeen individually every two to three weeks, with blocks of weekly therapy in term time.\n\nHearing:\nNormal. Audiology 3 September 2025, with normal tympanograms.\nNo history of ear infections.\nDental check in July 2026, with no problems.\n\nCognition:\nNon-verbal skills within the normal range on assessment by the educational psychologist in March 2026.\nThe diagnosis of developmental language disorder rests on his language profile and its impact on daily life, not on this result.\nFine and gross motor skills typical; rides a bike with stabilisers.\n\nAssessment, most recent, 14 August 2026:\nReceptive language — difficulty following multi-step instructions; he manages one step reliably and two with visual support.\nUnderstanding is better in small groups than in whole-class teaching.\nDifficulty understanding complex grammar, particularly passives and embedded clauses.\nExpressive language — short sentences of three to four words.\nSentences were mostly two words at the start of therapy.\nWord-finding difficulties with frequent use of \"that thing\".\nHe benefits from a first-sound cue, for example \"it starts with b\".\nImmature grammar, with errors in verb tense and plural marking.\nNarrative skills emerging but disorganised; he gives events out of sequence.\nRetells a familiar story better with picture prompts.\nVocabulary below age expectations, standard score 78.\nReceptive and expressive scores have each improved since the June 2025 baseline, though both remain below the average range.\n\nSpeech sounds:\nAge appropriate. No articulation or phonological therapy needed.\nSpeech is clear to unfamiliar listeners.\n\nSocial and behaviour:\nFriendly and engages well; seeks out adults and peers.\nSome frustration when misunderstood, occasionally expressed by walking away.\nSettles quickly when an adult gives him time and offers choices.\nNo behavioural concerns raised by his Reception class teacher.\nPlays happily with two close friends who are moving up to Year 1 with him.\nEnjoys drawing, trains and the climbing frame.\nHe is learning to swim at the local leisure centre.\n\nMedication:\nNone.\n\nAllergies:\nNone known.\n\nProgress and what works:\nGood response to visual supports — a visual timetable and picture cues.\nHe uses his visual timetable independently in Reception.\nBenefits from explicit vocabulary teaching, with new words pre-taught before a topic.\nLearns new words best with a picture, an action and several repetitions over a week.\nBenefits from repetition and from instructions chunked into single steps.\nResponds poorly to being asked \"do you understand?\", to which he always says yes.\nChecking understanding works better by asking him to show or tell back what he has to do.\nConcentrates best when seated near the front, away from the door.\n\nFamily:\nSupportive and consistent with the home programme.\nHis parents read with him each evening and use the vocabulary lists sent home.\nEnglish spoken at home.\nBoth parents work full time; his grandmother collects him from school three days a week.\nHis older brother had brief therapy for a lisp at four, now resolved.\nChildhood immunisations up to date; vision checked at school entry and normal.\nHis mother asked whether the school uniform shop opens during the holidays.\n\nPlan:\nHandover to the school-based service for classroom-embedded language support.\nThe community service is happy to attend a joint meeting with school in the first half-term.\nRecommend continued use of visual supports, pre-teaching of vocabulary, simplified and chunked instructions, and regular liaison with the class teacher.\nAnnual review of his progress suggested, with input from the class teacher and parents.\nPrevious assessment reports available on request; consent for sharing has been obtained.\nA copy of the most recent report has been given to his parents.\nHe is excited about Year 1 and has already visited his new classroom.\nHis Reception teacher has completed a handover meeting with the Year 1 teacher.\nHis parents asked whether he can bring a packed lunch instead of having school dinners.\n",
      "recipient": "The School Speech Pathologist, Greenfield Primary School",
