      "caseNotes": "\nPatient:\nMs Priya Sandhu, 34, primary school teacher.\nLives with her husband; no children.\nHer husband is a nurse working night shifts at the local hospital.\nShe grew up in Leicester and moved to the area for her first teaching post.\nSeen 29 August 2026 for initial voice assessment.\nReferred by her GP, who noticed the hoarseness at a routine appointment.\nThe new school term starts on 3 September 2026.\n\nPresenting complaint:\nProgressive hoarseness over five months.\nBegan during the spring term, with no preceding cold or chest infection.\nVocal fatigue by midday, with the voice deteriorating markedly through the school day and recovering partly overnight.\nThe summer holiday has brought some improvement, but her voice is still not back to normal.\nOccasional complete aphonia, most recently for two hours last week.\nHer voice returned each time after voice rest.\nHabitual throat clearing.\nShe is aware of the habit and finds it hard to stop.\nMild discomfort in the throat, described as an ache rather than pain.\nThe ache is worse at the end of the school week.\nNo dysphagia, no haemoptysis, no weight loss, no neck lump, no otalgia — no red flags.\n\nVocal demands:\nTeaches Year 2, thirty children.\nHas taught for eleven years, all at the same school.\nAlso runs the after-school choir twice weekly and coaches netball outdoors.\nShe has arranged for a colleague to lead the choir this autumn.\nNo amplification available in the classroom.\nHer classroom is next to the hall and is noisy during lunchtime clubs.\nShe speaks over background noise for much of the day.\n\nLifestyle:\nNever smoked.\nNo exposure to second-hand smoke at home.\nAlcohol minimal.\nUsually one glass of wine at the weekend.\nFour coffees daily.\nShe has started to swap two of these for water.\nHydration poor during teaching days; she reports drinking almost nothing between 8 am and lunchtime.\nSleeps about seven hours a night.\nRuns three times a week and attends a weekly yoga class.\n\nPast medical history:\nMild reflux, untreated, with occasional night-time symptoms.\nUsually after a late evening meal; no heartburn during the day.\nSeasonal allergic rhinitis with post-nasal drip.\nTonsillectomy aged seven.\nFractured right ankle skiing in 2019, fully healed.\nCervical screening up to date.\nHay fever is usually worst in June and settles by August.\n\nMedication:\nLoratadine 10 mg as required.\n\nAllergies:\nNone known.\n\nAssessment:\nPerceptual voice quality — rough and breathy, with reduced loudness and reduced pitch range.\nVoice quality worsened slightly over the session as she tired.\nHard glottal onsets on vowel-initial words.\nEasy onset achieved in trial words with modelling.\nHabitual throat clearing observed six times in a 20-minute session.\nClavicular breathing pattern with little abdominal movement.\nShe was able to copy abdominal breathing briefly with a model.\nMaximum phonation time 9 seconds, below normal.\nSpeaking pitch within the normal female range.\nVoice Handicap Index 42, in the moderate range.\nVisible neck and shoulder tension when speaking.\nSinging range reduced; she can no longer reach the higher notes with the choir.\nHearing normal on informal screening.\n\nTherapy provided:\nVocal hygiene advice given while awaiting the ENT examination; voice therapy proper will start once the larynx has been seen.\nShe was given written advice and a daily water plan, and understood the reason for each point.\nShe is motivated to change her habits and keen to begin therapy.\n\nImpression:\nSuspected bilateral vocal fold nodules, given the occupation, the pattern of symptoms and the perceptual findings.\nPolyps or cysts remain possible, which is a further reason for imaging.\nLaryngeal imaging has not yet been performed, which is the gap this referral addresses.\n\nPlan:\nRequesting laryngoscopy and stroboscopy to confirm the diagnosis and to guide further management; five months of hoarseness in any patient warrants visualisation of the larynx regardless of the likely benign cause.\nVoice therapy (resonant voice technique and breath support) to start after the laryngeal examination; vocal hygiene advice to continue meanwhile.\nReflux review suggested.\nShe would prefer to discuss reflux treatment with her GP.\nOccupational health referral discussed regarding classroom amplification.\nHer head teacher is supportive and has agreed to review her timetable.\nShe has a family wedding abroad in late October and hopes her voice will be better by then.\nShe asked whether the clinic offers early-morning appointments before school.\nShe can attend appointments at short notice during the autumn half-term week.\nShe asked whether there is a café near the clinic.\n",
      "recipient": "Dr Anita Rasheed, Ear, Nose and Throat Surgeon, Riverside ENT Clinic",
